{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means fewer distractions—not fewer comforts.** In a small flat, it’s about choosing a complete, useful set of furniture that feels coherent rather than crowded.\n\n- **Give each piece a purpose:** comfortable seating, a reachable table, lighting and enough storage—not an almost-empty room.\n- **Keep routes clear:** in your flat, protect the paths from the entrance to the living area, bedroom and balcony doors.\n- **Use a restrained palette:** warm white, one wood tone and a small amount of accent colour can feel calm without looking sterile.\n- **Reduce visual clutter:** closed storage and simple shapes help; texture in upholstery and a rug can add warmth.\n- **Choose fewer, well-proportioned pieces:** lots of tiny furniture can look busier than a few thoughtfully sized items.\n\nYour supplied scene has no furniture yet, so these are design principles—not an applied layout. The aim is **everything you need, with room to enjoy it.**", "conversationId": "267526f3ae28484ba87e531f5ba5a7d5"}
